.TH ZE 1 "August 2026" ze.sh "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
ze \- pattern-based navigation of a dynamically ranked directory stack
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY ze
.OP  \-cefhlrt
.RI [ pattern | pathname | \- ]
.
.SH AVAILABILITY
bash, zsh, ksh93, mksh, fish
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.P
\fBze.sh\fP is a shell-native directory jumper for \fBbash\fP, \fBzsh\fP,
\fBksh93\fP, and \fBmksh\fP. A wrapper for use with the
.B fish
shell is also provided.
.LP
Unlike its predecessor \fBz.sh\fP, \fBze.sh\fP uses an
exponential moving sum (EMS) scoring model on an event-driven clock (a global
monotonic event counter shared across all stored entries).
.B ze.sh
uses the
.B ze
command for all navigation, including pathname-based directory changes. If you
want to also track ordinary
.B cd
commands, alias
.B cd
to
.BR _ze_cd .
After a short learning phase, \fBze\fP will take you to the highest-scoring
directory that matches the given pattern. Patterns are interpreted as extended
regular expressions (ERE),
.TP
.B \-c
Restrict matches to subdirectories of the current directory.
.TP
.BI \-d " [-- fdopts] [pattern [path]]
Instead of querying the database, search the filesystem with
.B fd
to discover directories not yet recorded in
.BR ze 's
database. The directory to which to navigate is selected with
.B fzf
and recorded in the database. Note that
.BI ze " -d
uses
.BR fd 's
native query language. In particular, quoted blanks remain literal, unlike
ordinary
.BI ze " pattern
matching where blanks are translated to '.*' and match arbitrary intervening
characters.
.TP
.B \-e
Print the best match, do not \fBcd\fP.
.TP
.B \-f
Use \fBfzf\fP for interactive selection.
.TP
.B \-h
Show a brief help message.
.TP
.B \-l
List all matching directories by score.
.TP
.B \-r
Sort matches by visit count.
.TP
.B \-t
Sort matches by recency of last visit.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B ze
Change to \fI$HOME\fP.
.TP
.BI "ze " \-
Change to the previous directory.
.TP
.BI "ze " foo
Change to the absolute or relative pathname
.I foo
if it exists.
.P
.B "If pathname interpretation does not succeed:
.TP
.BI ze " foo"
Change to the highest-scoring directory matching \fIfoo\fP.
.TP
.BI ze " foo bar"
Change to the highest-scoring directory matching both \fIfoo\fP
and \fIbar\fP.
.TP
.BI "ze \-c " foo
Change to the highest-scoring subdirectory of \fI$PWD\fP matching
\fIfoo\fP.
.TP
.BI "ze \-l " foo
List all directories matching \fIfoo\fP, sorted by score. Without
argument, show full stack.
.TP
.BI "ze \-r " foo
Change to the most frequently visited directory matching
\fIfoo\fP.
.TP
.BI "ze \-t " foo
Change to the most recently visited directory matching \fIfoo\fP.
.P
.B "If fzf is available:
.TP
.BI "ze \-f " foo
Interactively select a matching directory using \fBfzf\fP. Without
argument, use full stack.
.P
.B "If fd is available, too:
.TP
.BI "ze \-d" " foo
Search file tree rooted in $PWD with
.B fd
for directories matching the \fBfd\fP pattern \fIfoo\fP. Bare
.B "ze \-d
searches for all directories. Options can be passed to \fBfd\fP by specifying
the end of options marker of \fBze\fP, e.g.:
.BI "ze \-d" " -- -d3"
to constrain the search depth to 3 levels. To search for all directories
in a path other than $PWD, use
.BI "ze -d" " . /path/to/root
or \fBze -d \fI'' /path/to/root\fR.
.
.SH INSTALLATION
.P
For native use,
put the following in your shell rc file (\fI.bashrc\fP, \fI.kshrc\fP, etc.):
.IP
source /path/to/ze.sh
.P
Optionally, to track ordinary \fBcd\fP commands while retaining native \fBcd\fP
semantics:
.IP
alias cd=_ze_cd
.P
For full \fBze\fP behaviour including pattern navigation on every \fBcd\fP
invocation, use \fB_ZE_CMD=cd\fP instead (see CONFIGURATION).
.P
For
.BR fish ,
install ze.sh, zex.sh, and the wrapper functions in suitable locations, e.g.
.LP
.EX
    cp ze.sh zex.sh ~/bin   # or any other directory on your $PATH
    chmod +x ~/bin/zex.sh
    cp contrib/fish/ze.fish contrib/fish/_ze_cd.fish ~/.config/fish/functions/
.EE
.LP
Note that ze.sh and zex.sh need to reside in the same directory.
.LP
To track \fBcd\fP or shadow it with full \fBze\fP behaviour under fish, add one
of the following to \fI~/.config/fish/config.fish\fP:
.IP
alias cd _ze_cd
.IP
alias cd ze
.LP
Use \fBze\fP and aliased \fBcd\fP with pathname-based navigation for some time to
build up the database. Afterwards use
.B ze
for pattern-based navigation of the directories known to the database.
.SH DESIGN
.P
\fBze.sh\fP differs from \fBz.sh\fP in several ways:
.TP
.B event clock
\fBze.sh\fP uses an event clock (each newly recorded \fBcd\fP event
advances the clock by one tick) instead of wall-clock time for score decay. This
prevents score decay during shell inactivity, especially during long periods (e.g.
holidays).
.TP
.B scoring
\fBze.sh\fP uses an exponential moving sum (EMS) scoring
model. The score is the sum of exponentially decayed unit impulses at each visit
time. The decay rate is controlled by \fB_ZE_LAMBDA\fP (default: \fB8e-3\fP).
.TP
.B tracking
\fBze.sh\fP only tracks explicit \fBze\fP-based directory changes
(or bare \fBcd\fP if aliased to \fB_ze_cd\fP). It does not use precommand hooks.
.
.SH DATABASE
.P
Data is stored in \fB~/.ze/ze.db\fP as a plain text file with one entry per line:
.IP
\fIpath|visits|ticks|score\fP
.P
where:
.TP
\fIpath\fP
Absolute directory path.
.TP
\fIvisits\fP
Cumulative visit count for this entry.
.TP
\fIticks\fP
Global cumulative \fBcd\fP event count at time of last visit.
.TP
\fIscore\fP
Exponentially decayed cumulative visit score as of the last visit.
.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.P
The following environment variables can be set before sourcing ze.sh (typically in
your shell rc):
.TP
\fB_ZE_CMD\fP
Command name (default: \fBze\fP). Must be set before sourcing ze.sh so that tab
completion is registered under the chosen name. For example, \fB_ZE_CMD=myze\fP
makes \fBmyze\fP the command name with working tab completion. Setting
\fB_ZE_CMD=cd\fP shadows the builtin \fBcd\fP with full \fBze\fP behaviour
including pattern navigation, changing \fBcd\fP semantics accordingly.
.TP
\fB_ZE_DIR\fP
Database directory (default: \fB~/.ze\fP).
.TP
\fB_ZE_LAMBDA\fP
Decay constant in units of 1/cd-action (default: \fB8e-3\fP).
.TP
\fB_ZE_DBMAX\fP
Database size limit (default: \fB640\fP).
.TP
\fB_ZE_OWNER\fP
Allow use on shared databases by specifying the owner username.
.TP
\fB_ZE_RESOLVE_SYMLINKS\fP
Set to any non-empty value to resolve symlinks to physical paths (default: logical
paths are honoured).
.TP
\fB_ZE_EXCLUDE_DIRS\fP Array of directory trees to exclude from tracking.
.
.SH FZF INTEGRATION
.P
If \fBfzf\fP is installed,
.BI "ze \-f " [pattern]
opens an interactive selector showing all matching directories ranked by score,
with the best match at the top of the \fBfzf\fP display.
.
.SH MIGRATING FROM Z.SH
.P
If you have an existing \fB~/.z\fP database from \fBz.sh\fP, you can convert it
for use with \fBze.sh\fP:
.P
.EX
mkdir -p ~/.ze
sort -t'|' -k3,3n ~/.z | awk -F'|' '
    BEGIN { OFS = FS; lambda = 8e-3 }
    {
        visits = $2
        ticks += visits
        if (visits == 1) {
            score = 1
        } else {
            step = ticks/visits
            decay_step = exp(-lambda * step)
            decay_full = exp(-lambda * ticks)
            score = (1 - decay_full)/(1 - decay_step)
        }
        print $1, visits, ticks, score
    }' > ~/.ze/ze.db
.EE
.P
.
.SH FILES
.P
\fB~/.ze/ze.db\fP \- Default database file.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.P
The following shell functions are defined in the global namespace after sourcing
\fBze.sh\fP:
.TP
\fB_ze\fP
Main function, aliased to the command name specified by \fB_ZE_CMD\fP (default: \fBze\fP).
.TP
\fB_ze_cd\fP
cd wrapper. Alias \fBcd\fP to this function to track ordinary cd commands.
.TP
\fB_ze_commit\fP, \fB_ze_complete\fP, \fB_ze_dig\fP, \fB_ze_dirs\fP, \fB_ze_fzf\fP, \fB_ze_record\fP
Internal functions, not intended for direct use.
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.P
re_format(7), awk(1), grep(1), fd(1), fzf(1)
.P
Project repository: https://github.com/jghub/ze
.
.SH NOTES
.TP
.B "Smartcase matching
Pattern matching is case-insensitive unless the pattern contains uppercase
characters.
.TP
.B "Stale entries
Entries for directories that no longer exist are retained in the database and
filtered at match time. They may be pruned if the database exceeds the size
limit.
.TP
.B Symlinks
By default, logical paths are honoured. Set \fB_ZE_RESOLVE_SYMLINKS\fP to resolve
symlinks to physical paths.
